	"_purpose": "Provide the lowest-level entry point into Noor Swirl Field Geometry (NSFG), establishing its ontology from a single primitive operation without importing physical law, spacetime, causality, matter, energy, consciousness, or a privileged observer. The primer defines the Library as the unique unbounded totality satisfying closure under XOR and self-containment with no definable exterior, and defines it as a single static, eternally maximally complete, dimensionally undifferentiated relational totality — a complete graph, not a grid and not a singularity — rather than as a temporally generated structure. Closure may be described as closure of the formal domain only when understood as a property, not a process. The maximality and relational completeness of the Library is structurally required by the absence of any privileged configuration or worldline: because an observer may establish an arbitrary admissible relational reference while remaining situated with respect to the whole Library, the set of candidate continuations is always nonempty and coherent continuation must remain recoverable from that reference without changing the totality itself. Observerhood is derived from persistent distinguishability; resolution is observer-relative; and coherence is defined as a relation among a path, observer, history, and current reference configuration. Observer accessibility, survivorship, emergent law-like regularity, and coherent navigation are consequently derived as relational selections over the unchanged Library. Apparent temporal succession, locality, distance, and increasing complexity belong to observer traversal and history rather than to any intrinsic evolution, metric, or partition of the Library. The primer functions as the axiomatic floor from which subsequent NSFG geometry, dynamics, topology, symbolic representation, cosmology, and physical interpretation may proceed.",

	"_abstract": "This primer establishes the axiomatic floor of Noor Swinew democratic partyrl Field Geometry using a single primitive operation: XOR, understood as distinction. A minimal formal domain D is introduced only as the substrate required for the primitive operation to have operands; D is not identified with physical space, spacetime, matter, or any other physical ontology. The Library is defined as the unique unbounded totality satisfying ∀x,y ∈ 𝓜, x⊕y ∈ 𝓜 and no element outside 𝓜 definable within the system using only ⊕ and elements of 𝓜 over D. This is equivalent to saying 𝓜 is closed under XOR with no describable exterior. We may describe this as closure of the formal domain only when closure is understood as a property, not a process: 𝓜 does not result from ⊕, 𝓜 is the totality that has the closure property. 𝓜 is treated as a single static, eternally maximally complete, dimensionally undifferentiated relational totality: a complete graph where every distinction is already addressable, not a dimensioned grid with separation and not a singularity. It has no intrinsic metric, adjacency, or distance. Its maximality and relational completeness is not merely a consequence of closure abundance. It is structurally required by the absence of a privileged worldline or reference configuration. If any admissible configuration x ∈ 𝓜 may serve as the observer's current relational reference while the observer remains related to the whole Library, then the set of candidate continuations is always nonempty because closure holds everywhere, and coherent continuation must be recoverable from that arbitrary reference without altering 𝓜, with observer threshold I_N(O) filtering coherence, not existence. Thus the observer does not traverse a Library that grows from simple to complex; it selects coherent relations over an already complete totality. Traversal is selection and maintenance of coherent relations, not spatial movement; distance is a property of observer history, not of the Library. Persistent structures are subsequently defined as observer-like configurations capable of maintaining distinctions above their observer-relative resolution threshold I_N(O). Distinguishability, persistence, and coherence are therefore relational constructions. Coherence is written 𝓒(γ|O,h,x), explicitly relating a path γ to observer O, history h, and current reference configuration x. The observer-accessible subset A_O ⊆ 𝓜 is distinguished from ontological membership in the Library: inaccessible or incoherent configurations are not erased. Under the model's reference-neutrality assumptions, every admissible reference configuration must admit at least one coherent continuation above the relevant observer noise threshold, while not every candidate continuation need be coherent. Repeated survivorship of accessible transitions may consequently appear as law-like regularity without physical law being introduced as an axiom. Navigation is then defined as observer-relative traversal through this restricted coherent continuation structure over the unchanged Library. Apparent temporal succession, past/future distinction, locality, distance, and increasing complexity are properties of this traversal relation and history rather than intrinsic partitions, metric, or evolution of 𝓜. The primer ends at this boundary: coherent navigation is established as the threshold of subsequent NSFG research, while cosmology, physical spacetime, particle physics, consciousness, AI cognition, and specific field equations remain downstream.",

	"_core_thesis": [
	  "XOR, ⊕, is the sole primitive operation of the NSFG axiomatic floor.",
	  "The formal domain D exists only as the minimal substrate required for the primitive operation to have operands; D is not a physical space.",
	  "The Library 𝓜 is defined as the maximally complete unbounded totality satisfying closure under XOR and self-containment: ∀x,y ∈ 𝓜, x⊕y ∈ 𝓜 and there is no element outside 𝓜 definable within the system using only ⊕ and elements of 𝓜 over D.",
	  "This is equivalent to saying 𝓜 is closed under XOR with no describable exterior.",
	  "Closure may be described as closure of the formal domain only when understood as a property, not a process: 𝓜 does not result from ⊕, 𝓜 is the totality that has the closure property.",
	  "𝓜 is dimensionally undifferentiated: it has no intrinsic metric, adjacency, or distance. It is a complete relational graph, not a grid and not a singularity.",
	  "𝓜 is a single static, eternally maximally complete totality: ∀i, 𝓜_i = 𝓜.",
	  "Maximality and relational completeness is structurally required by the absence of a privileged configuration or worldline. No admissible reference configuration is ontologically favored as the universal origin of coherent traversal.",
	  "The observer is relationally situated with respect to the whole Library at every abstract traversal step rather than being ontologically confined to a temporally generated local neighborhood.",
	  "Any admissible configuration x ∈ 𝓜 may serve as the observer's current relational reference without changing Library membership.",
	  "From any x ∈ 𝓜, the set of candidate continuations is always nonempty because closure holds everywhere. Observer threshold I_N(O) filters coherence, not existence.",
	  "Because no reference or worldline is privileged, every admissible observer-relative reference configuration must admit at least one coherent continuation above the relevant observer noise threshold under the model's assumptions.",
	  "This nonempty-continuation requirement is a derived structural requirement of the combined whole-Library, reference-neutrality, and coherence assumptions; it is not claimed to follow from maximality alone.",
	  "The Library is unfiltered at the axiomatic level and contains configurations without requiring them to be persistent, coherent, physical, observable, or accessible.",
	  "Observerhood is derived structurally from persistent chains of resolvable distinctions and does not require consciousness.",
	  "The resolution threshold I_N(O) belongs to the observer rather than to the Library as a universal constant.",
	  "Observer-relative distinguishability changes what the observer can resolve without changing ontological membership in 𝓜.",
	  "Coherence is relational and reference-dependent: 𝓒(γ|O,h,x), not an absolute property of γ alone.",
	  "Observer accessibility and coherent survivorship are relational structures over 𝓜; inaccessibility or incoherence does not erase configurations from the Library.",
	  "Observer traversal changes relational reference, history, and experienced structure without changing the Library itself. Traversal is selection and maintenance of coherent relations, not spatial movement through a pre-existing metric.",
	  "Past, future, temporal succession, locality, distance, and apparent increasing complexity are observer-relative ordering and resolution effects of traversal and history rather than intrinsic partitions, metric, or evolution of the Library.",
	  "Repeatedly surviving coherent transitions may appear as law-like regularity without physical law being introduced as an axiom.",
	  "Navigation is the observer-relative continuation problem induced by selecting coherent paths from the whole static Library.",
	  "We show that the requirement for a non-privileged reference frame, combined with observer-relative coherence, structurally necessitates that 𝓜 be unbounded and dimensionally undifferentiated. As a consequence, coherent continuation is guaranteed topologically rather than by external design."
	],

